A||N||13||Monkeyman||Shuddering||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-19-2001||07:36 PM||||I get a shuddering if my car ('86 GT/4spd but originally a Getrag) is coasting.  If I'm on the gas, it's OK.  This just started a few weeks ago.  Any ideas what it could be?  Seems to be worse when it's cold, but that could be partly my imagination.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PontiacAPE.jpg">    <BR>Monkeyman||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||09-19-2001||10:29 PM||||Rough idle while moving?  Maybe worn motor mounts or loose bolts that allow the engine/tranny to move around more when there isnt as much force applied?  Herd of wildebeest chasing after you?<P>What exactly feels like it's shaking?  A front end shimmy?  Rear end rattle?  Or just a whole car shake...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||09-19-2001||10:45 PM||||Rear end/drivetrain.  Almost a surging, but not.  Kind of like the feeling you get when you have a badly warped brake rotor.  (This is not the problem.  It feels different than that.  I'm just using this as an example.)  Imagine two metal plates.  One is attached to the motor, the other to the transmission.  Then imagine that both of them are warped and rubbing against each other.  This is what I imagine when I feel the "shuddering".  Once I'm on the gas (any time I'm not coasting), everything smoothes out.  The car idles fine, if that matters.<P>I could just bring the car down to the local dealership for an evaluation.  They've been awfully good to me, but I've spent so much money there since I've had the car, I just can't afford it right now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||09-19-2001||10:51 PM||||Dave, I've got the same thing with my SE. I bet its the dogbone. Yours is rubber right? I've got a tired(looks good) stock rubber dogbone in my SE, and that is the problem. My Formula had it until I put poly in. Try a new(or poly)dogbone and that should fix you up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||09-20-2001||12:42 AM||||Hey Monkeyman my did the same thing.  My friend has a poly dogbone and we put it on and it stopped.  He took the dogbone back and I had to put back on the stock one and it will do it again.  So I am getting a poly one here very soon.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||05:59 AM||||Absolutely can't be a worn out dogbone as I just replaced mine with the Ultimate Dog Bone.  (Why don't they paint those red?)  Could this mean I might have a bad motor/tranny mount?  Someone local suggested that I might need to have my flywheel resurfaced.  I'm not really sure what a flywheel does, but it sounds expensive.||||reg||1
Z||000006||c m west||09-20-2001||06:02 AM||||sounds as if the clutch is worn and the springs on the disk are beggining to fatigue||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||06:30 AM||||Is this something I can live with for the next 5k-10k miles (mostly in town driving)?  I don't have an extra $600 lying around to replace the clutch right now.  Is there anything I could do to lessen the effect without any major work?  I can live with a little bit of embarrassment for a while, but this is my daily driver.||||reg||1
Z||000008||c m west||09-20-2001||06:35 AM||||ummmmm - take the bus?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||07:05 AM||||Am I in that much trouble?  How long could I expect something like this to last before I have no choice but to take the bus (we don't have any buses here, but I still have a rusty old bike out in the garage somewhere)?||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroJoe||09-20-2001||10:26 AM||||Judging by c m west's responces it sounds like the clutch is almost "bouncing" off the flywheel.  The flywheel is attached to the crankshaft of the engine, it is what spins and gives the power to the tranny.  In manual trasmission cars, the surface of the flywheel is machined smooth, so the clutch plate (surface like that of a brake pad, a wearable part) can attach to it and transmit the power to the tranny and then to the wheels.<P>I don't know how long you can last like that, you might just want to take short trips and keep a cell phone with you at all times.  Hate to say it that way but your clutch may be on its last legs.<P>One thing to try though, is just check the motor mounts.  See if any are loose, or exceptionally worn.  Just try to rule out all the possibilities that you can before worrying about the clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||09-20-2001||12:47 PM||||Dave, it could be worn engine/tranny mounts. I had poly dogbone and worn engine mount on my Formula, and had the same problem(GM sucks when it comes to mounting the engine/tranny in their cars). My problem was also worse when cold(and my clutch only had 10K at the time).<P>Also, if it is the clutch Monkey, I'm sure you could find a fellow forum member who could replace the clutch set for you for less than $600 including parts.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-20-2001||06:08 PM||||Odd as it sounds, it seems to be better today.  Not gone entirely, but better.  I'll check out those engine/tranny mounts this weekend, Drew.  Can I replace those just by jacking up the motor slightly?  Or will I need to drop the cradle?||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||09-20-2001||07:32 PM||||You can replace the mounts by using a jack. I've done the mounts on both of mine. The front tranny mount is a PITA(!!!) to replace, other than that, it isn't too terrible. I give it a 4-5 in ease, and a 7 in PITA. Be thankful you've got a pre-88, the '88 engine mount is the worst of them all to replace.||||reg||1
